  m_text: Platypuses are most active at night, from sunset to sunrise. When they
    dive for dinner, they close their eyes, ears and nostrils, so they can't use
    sight, hearing or smell. Instead, they use electroreceptors in their bill to
    pick up the tiny electrical signals made by prey like shrimp, worms and insect
    larvae. They sweep their bill side to side like a metal detector to find food
    in the dark, murky water.
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
